WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.780
- The intern was like, 'I
think he's bleeding out,'

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.310
and literally, it was like
a waterfall of blood just

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.963
coming out through the pelvis.

00:00:08.246 --> 00:00:10.913
(pensive music)

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.210
- What's the craziest thing
you've seen in the ER?

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.490
- Two people came in
through the ambulance bay,

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.660
and they were Smurf Blue.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.493
I was like, 'woah.'

00:00:19.493 --> 00:00:20.670
I was like, 'that's interesting.'

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.100
I was like, 'whats that all about?'

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.630
- A guy with the thing
you use for guacamole,

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:25.463
I guess to grind it.

00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:27.253
I don't know what that thing's called.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.250
He put that up his rectum and...

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.510
We see people sticking stuff
up their butts maybe every

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:33.720
once every couple months.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.033
- What's the worst smell
you've experienced?

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.170
- I don't know if you wanna hear this,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.980
but I guess bloody diarrhea.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:39.813
Does that count?

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.763
- You know, it's so hard because
when you do it every day,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.253
you're used to it.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.393
I recently smelled a really pungent vomit.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.750
I've smelled vomit before,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.513
but I don't know why this
time it just really hit me.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.363
What still grosses you out?

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.026
- Body odor still really bothers me.

00:00:57.026 --> 00:00:58.360
So, when I get those patients,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.770
I kinda just throw on a mask and I'm like,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.650
'oh, I'm sorry, I'm coughing,
I'm not feeling well,'

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.220
but it's really because
they smell really bad.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.679
- So, I don't get grossed out by things,

00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:09.700
but when I hear the story, though,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.110
of how some people get
hurt, that grosses me out.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.483
- Has an incident ever freaked you out?

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.780
- I remember I passed by a
room and one patient actually

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.933
was already somewhat restrained,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.230
but had monster strength
and just came out of...

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.760
He lifted the whole stretcher up.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:31.940
I was like, 'is that real?'

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.224
- There was a guy that came in one time

00:01:33.224 --> 00:01:34.730
and I unwrapped his foot

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.360
and there was maggots all over his foot,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.428
and it was my job, lucky me,
to go and clean up the maggots,

00:01:39.428 --> 00:01:42.640
and the maggots pretty
much ate down to the bone.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.260
- How often do you have
to look up a diagnosis?

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.150
- Every day.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.919
I mean, things are changing all the time.

00:01:47.919 --> 00:01:51.443
- You see a lot of the
same things all the time,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.190
and then once in a blue
moon you'll get a...

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.633
You'll get something that
you don't know at all.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.980
The other doctors might
actually talk amongst each other

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.630
and ask each other, 'oh,
what do you think this is?'

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.430
and they bounce off of each other.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.900
- Definitely when I first
started out, I was like,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.740
'oh, yeah, I definitely know
what you're talking about.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.507
I'm just gonna go over there
just to get your medication.'

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:10.943
Yeah.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.973
- What's the most rare condition
or disease you've seen?

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.113
She had this fainting condition.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.050
We had to Google it, and it was real.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.240
It was a real thing,
and I'd never seen it,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.920
but she had something
that said I always faint,

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.123
this is normal for me.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.403
I faint and, you know,
this happens all the time.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.280
- This was a baby like six months ago,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.370
and he had this condition
where his heart just wasn't

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:36.610
working properly,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.960
and he was gonna need a heart transplant

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.883
at six months to survive.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.370
We coded him for a while, and then we...

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.268
We sent him to another hospital.

00:02:45.268 --> 00:02:47.440
I don't know what happened
to him after that,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.040
but that was probably one
of the most rare conditions

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.040
I've seen in the ER.

00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:54.019
(exhaling sharply)
Sorry.

00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:57.817
(crew member mumbling)

00:03:00.282 --> 00:03:01.499
(clears throat)

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:02.463
Next one.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.660
Do you eventually become numb to trauma?

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.000
- One of the things that actually

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.460
a lot of us have tried to do
is that if a patient passes,

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.160
we'll actually pause at
the end of the trauma

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.200
and actually have a moment of silence

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.550
to just honor the patient.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.590
That makes you more conscious
of what you're doing,

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.170
and it's just not just another
person coming through a door.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.230
- When the patient first comes,

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.880
you kinda have to be numb
to just get through the day

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.030
and get through whatever
you need to get through,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.100
but then once all of it's
done, you're like, 'oh my gosh,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.719
I can't believe that just
happened to somebody.'

00:03:27.719 --> 00:03:29.500
Anything could happen
to anyone at any time,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.470
so we have a appreciation
for life after each shift

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:33.690
where we talk to each other about it.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:34.720
- You know what I call it, though?

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.220
I really think ER nurses
are a jack of all trades,

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.750
and that's why I'm so proud to
be an ER nurse in particular

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.100
because I think we do so
many different things.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.693
So, I mean, I love it.

